{"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 4: PROCYON models domains, peptides, and small molecules beyond proteins. a) PROCYON predicts the small molecule binding domain on proteins, where the input is the description and molecular structure of a drug, and the output is a ranked list of domains on a target protein. In this example, PROCYON retrieves domains of MGAM given the drug miglitol and correctly prioritizes the binding domain for miglitol as the first among the MGAM's nine domains. Each domain is identified by the Pfam ID and the index of the first amino acid in the domain. b) PROCYON ranks the correct drug-binding domain (highlighted in orange) on respective target proteins highly. In Q06187, the green domain ( PF00017 281 ) denotes the PROCYON-predicted binding domain, but the orange domain ( PF07714 402 ) is the correct binding domain. PROCYON predicts the binding domain as top-ranked in 24 examples, second-ranked in 5 examples, and so on. c) PROCYON can be finetuned to predict protein-peptide binding. Left, PROCYON is first finetuned on naturally occurring protein-peptide complexes from the Protein Data Bank (PDB), then tested on an experimental dataset of synthetic peptides screened for binding to the ACE2 protein. Right, the predicted binding scores for binders versus non-binders. Two-sided Mann-Whitney U test. d) Leveraging PROCYON to perform indication-specific drug target retrieval. In this example, the input consists of a novel task definition, the description of indications (nicotine addiction or major depressive disorder), and the name and structure of bupropion. PROCYON ranks known targets (norepinephrine transporter (NET), dopamine transporter (DAT), and nicotinic acetylcholinergic receptor (AChR)) of bupropion among the top 40/18,174 human proteins. Shown are 95% confidence intervals derived by perturbing words in the input prompt. ** p -value < 0 . 001 ; two-sided Wilcoxon signed rank test.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure labeled **a-d** describing **ProCyon**, a model/prompting framework for protein-related prediction tasks.\n\nPanel **a** shows a protein structure labeled **O43451 (MGAM)**, with an **orange binding domain** highlighted. The workflow says the protein is **decomposed into domains**, then an **input prompt** is constructed. The prompt includes a task description asking whether text describes a drug and which domains bind the drug, an input text about **miglitol**, a drug structure, and a list of domains for MGAM. The output shown as **ProCyon** is a ranked domain table with scores; top domains include **PF01055_349** and **PF01055_2111**.\n\nPanel **b** shows three protein examples with structures and drug targets: **P00742 / Apixaban**, **Q13451 / Sirolimus**, and **O05187 / Ibrutinib**. Each has a domain-score table. A bar chart below reports the **number of protein-drug binding examples** by predicted domain rank, with counts **24, 5, 4, 2, 0** for ranks 1-5, indicating many examples have the binding domain ranked first.\n\nPanel **c** shows a **ProCyon-Bind** training/testing setup. Training uses **natural protein-peptide complexes**; testing uses **synthetic ACE2 peptide binders**, including a synthetic peptide generation schematic and an **ACE2 protein** structure. A violin plot compares predicted binding scores for **Peptide ACE2 binder** vs **Peptide ACE2 non-binder**, with a reported **p = 1.04e-4**, suggesting higher scores for binders.\n\nPanel **d** shows text-based biomedical/drug prompt examples for **Smoking cessation** and **Major depressive disorder**. Inputs contain disease descriptions and drug structures, including **Bupropion**. Downstream diagrams link predicted **disease** and **drug information** to **18.7k human proteins**, with a ranked comparison plot for MDD showing methods labeled **NET**, **DAT**, and **AChR**, and significance brackets."]}

